+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+Date: 4 November 2026  
+Scope: the production risks visible in this repository, including the service API, persistence, scheduled payment
+dispatch and its scheme, fraud, ledger and notification dependencies.
+
+The scores are on the usual 1–10 scale. Severity (S) is 1 = negligible customer or operational effect and 10 = a
+material financial, regulatory, security or safety impact. Occurrence (O) is 1 = unlikely and 10 = frequent or already
+present by design. Detection (D) is 1 = almost certain to be detected before impact and 10 = unlikely to be detected
+until a customer, dependant or reconciliation process finds it. RPN is `S × O × D`; higher values should be addressed
+first. These are current-risk scores, before the recommended actions are implemented.
+
+## Failure modes
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Cause | Effect on customers and dependant systems/teams | Detection | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action | 
+|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| FMEA-01 | An accepted payment is sent without fraud screening | `PaymentService` calls `PaymentDispatcher` directly; the available `FraudScreeningClient` is not wired into the payment path. | A payment that should have been held or rejected can leave the bank. Customers may suffer fraud; Fraud, Operations, Finance and the regulator face an avoidable incident. | Compare every scheme instruction with a fraud decision; alert on any missing decision; release-gate test for ACCEPT/REFER/REJECT and timeout paths. | 10 | 10 | 9 | **900** | Make screening a mandatory state transition before dispatch. Persist the decision and screening timestamp, fail closed on timeout/error, set REFER to `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, and prevent any bypass path. |
+| FMEA-02 | Scheme accepts an instruction but the local payment disappears or returns an error | The scheme can accept before its 10-second response times out. `SchemeTimeoutException` is not handled by the dispatcher, so the transaction rolls back; there is no status query or recovery. | The payee may receive money while the customer sees failure and retries. The local record, customer history and the 07:00 Finance reconciliation no longer match the scheme settlement report. | Reconcile scheme settlement to local payment references; monitor 5xx/timeouts and unmatched scheme instructions; investigate customer reports of duplicate or missing payments. | 10 | 4 | 8 | **320** | Treat timeout as `SUBMITTED`/`UNKNOWN`, retain the payment and query `GET /v2/instructions/{reference}`. Use `P` + payment id for all recovery and provide an operations exception queue. |
+| FMEA-03 | A scheme payment is sent but the ledger posting is absent or ambiguous | Ledger times out after the debit may have been booked. The service marks `POSTING_FAILED`; it has no automated status query and the customer can be told `PAYMENT_SENT` despite the ledger exception. | The customer view can disagree with core money records; Finance must repair a break and Operations must repost. A customer may believe a payment completed when the bank has not booked it. | Daily reconciliation of scheme settlement to ledger by payment reference; alert on `POSTING_FAILED`, ledger timeout rate and age of exceptions. | 10 | 5 | 7 | **350** | Keep and expose the original `ledgerReference`, query or confirm ledger status before repair, and make the daily exceptions report a monitored queue with ownership and ageing/SLA alerts. Notify using a distinct pending/failed template rather than unconditional SENT. |
+| FMEA-04 | A timed-out ledger posting is retried with a new reference and debits twice | Manual or future automatic retry does not reuse the payment's original idempotency key; the code generates a UUID when dispatching. | Customer is charged twice; the core ledger and scheme reconciliation break, requiring financial remediation and a complaint response. | Match ledger postings by payment id and amount; alert on more than one ledger reference per payment and reconciliation breaks. | 10 | 3 | 7 | **210** | Generate and persist `ledgerReference` before the first post, enforce one reference per payment, and make retry tooling reject a different reference. Add an integration test for timeout-before/after-booking. |
+| FMEA-05 | Accepted or queued payments are dispatched more than once | Repeated job execution, multiple service instances, concurrent requests, or a retry after an unclear response can process the same `QUEUED` payment without a claim/lock. | Duplicate scheme instructions, duplicate debits or confusing statuses; Finance and Operations must investigate. | Alert on duplicate scheme reference, duplicate ledger reference and more than one dispatch attempt; inspect job-run and payment-state metrics. | 10 | 4 | 7 | **280** | Add an atomic claim/lease or row lock and an explicit dispatch-attempt state. Make both scheme and ledger calls idempotent with the persisted references, and ensure only one scheduler instance processes a payment. |
+| FMEA-06 | A weekend or bank-holiday payment is sent on the wrong date and rejected | `WorkingDays` excludes weekends but has no UK bank-holiday calendar. The scheme rejects an invalid date with `DT01`. | Customer sees a rejection or delayed payment instead of the promised next working day; the app, support team and payment operations receive avoidable exceptions. | Alert on `DT01`, compare execution dates with the authoritative UK holiday calendar, and report queued payments past their execution date. | 7 | 5 | 5 | **175** | Use an authoritative, versioned UK bank-holiday calendar shared with the scheme; test year boundaries and observed holidays. Preserve the queue and reschedule `DT01` safely rather than losing the payment. |
+| FMEA-07 | Queued payments are not dispatched on time | Scheduling is a single 06:00 weekday job with no visible health/heartbeat, and it depends on service availability and a working database. | Customers miss expected payment timing; Operations receives overdue `QUEUED` items and downstream settlement is late. | Alert when the job heartbeat is missing, when queued items are past `executionDate`, and on queue age/count; provide an operator-run replay. | 8 | 5 | 6 | **240** | Add scheduler heartbeat, metrics and an alerting SLO; make processing resumable and idempotent, and expose a secured replay/re-drive operation with an audit trail. |
+| FMEA-08 | Payment approval checks use a stale or concurrently changed balance | The account balance is a copy overwritten by the ledger at 02:00. Payment creation reads and checks it without a ledger check or locking; two requests can pass the same balance/daily-limit test. | Overpayment, overdraft-like customer view and ledger exceptions; closing or reconciliation decisions can be wrong. Customers, Finance, Support and Operations are affected. | Reconcile account copies to the 02:00 ledger feed; alert on negative/rapidly inconsistent balances and ledger posting failures; concurrency tests. | 9 | 6 | 7 | **378** | Use the ledger for authoritative funds checks where required, or implement a clearly bounded reservation model. Add optimistic/pessimistic locking around balance and daily-limit accounting and reject stale copies. |
+| FMEA-09 | An account is closed with funds remaining, or closure is approved without the required controls | Closure requests check only local account status and there is no approval/closure implementation in this API; the knowledge bundle requires a zero-balance check against the ledger and a second approver. | Customer funds can be stranded; the account lifecycle, Support, Finance and Operations records diverge. A missing approval path leaves requests pending indefinitely. | Daily report of pending/approved closures; reconcile closure state to ledger balance and account status; audit review of requester versus approver. | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Implement an explicit approver-only decision endpoint and state machine, enforce separation of duties, query the ledger for zero balance immediately before closure, and make closure transition plus audit record atomic. |
+| FMEA-10 | One tenant or role can read or change another tenant's data | A forged/misissued token, missing tenant claim validation, an unsafe future endpoint, or a resource query that omits tenant filtering can bypass the otherwise central `AccessPolicy`. | Confidential customer data, internal notes/risk information or payment details may be disclosed; unauthorized payments and a reportable data breach can result. Support, Compliance and every tenant are affected. | Cross-tenant and role-matrix integration tests; security logs for denied requests; periodic query/code review and penetration testing. | 10 | 2 | 8 | **160** | Validate issuer, audience, required `sub`/`tenant` claims and role/scope combinations; require tenant predicates at repository boundaries; keep 404 masking and add tests for every endpoint, including payment lookup and closure paths. |
+| FMEA-11 | Staff-only information or payment data is exposed to a customer | A new projection/endpoint may serialize entities directly, or a response may include internal notes, comments, risk score or more payment data than intended. The payment view currently includes full payee account details by design, so handling and logging are sensitive. | Privacy breach, customer harm and regulatory reporting; complaints and Compliance must investigate. Notification content is also a breach if it contains a full account number or sort code. | API schema/contract tests by role; DLP scanning of responses, logs and notification payloads; review notification failures and templates. | 9 | 3 | 7 | **189** | Use allow-listed DTOs, never entity serialization, redact sensitive values in logs, enforce role-specific projections, and keep notifications to last four digits only. Add automated assertions that internal fields and full account numbers cannot leave the service. |
+| FMEA-12 | Valid customers cannot use the API or the service becomes unavailable | Database, JVM, network, dependency or signing-key failure; there is no visible resilience, health/SLO instrumentation or documented degradation path in the application. | App and online banking cannot show balances, statements or cases; payments and support work stop. Back-office, Fraud, Finance and Complaints lose access during the outage. | `/actuator/health`, request error/latency metrics, dependency probes, synthetic customer journeys and the 99.9% availability SLO. | 8 | 4 | 3 | **96** | Add production-grade readiness/liveness and dependency health signals, dashboards and paging; define DB backup/restore and key-rotation runbooks; use bounded timeouts/circuit breakers where safe and test recovery. |
+| FMEA-13 | Notification failure is treated as payment failure, or the customer is told the wrong status | Notifications are best effort, but the dispatcher sends the same `PAYMENT_SENT` notification for `POSTING_FAILED`; the notification client has no timeout configured and no retry/dead-letter path. | A payment may be correct but the customer is uninformed, or the customer is told it was sent when ledger posting is unresolved. Support receives avoidable contacts. | Monitor notification latency/error logs and compare notification events with payment states; sample templates and customer journeys. | 6 | 4 | 5 | **120** | Set a bounded notification timeout, emit durable outbox events after the payment state is committed, retry with a dead-letter queue, and use status-specific templates (`SENT`, `UNDER_REVIEW`, `FAILED`). |
+| FMEA-14 | Statements or the monthly regulatory return are incomplete or wrong | The repository contains statement records but no visible generation/cut process; statements depend on ledger closing balances and the return is due by the fifth working day. | Customers receive missing/wrong statements; Regulatory Reporting submits an incorrect return and Compliance must remediate. | Reconcile statement population and balances to ledger; completeness check before the fifth working day; customer/support defect trends. | 9 | 4 | 8 | **288** | Implement an idempotent, ledger-sourced statement cut with a run record and completeness gate; alert on missing accounts/periods and require Finance/Regulatory sign-off before submission. |
+| FMEA-15 | Audit timestamps and business cut-offs are wrong | Some domain objects use `Instant.now()` directly (`ClosureRequest`, `SupportCase`) while payment cut-offs use the injected UK clock; clock skew or inconsistent timezone handling changes ordering and eligibility. | Wrong payment day, misleading complaint/audit chronology and missed regulatory or operational SLAs. | Compare host clocks; test around 15:30, midnight, DST and holidays; detect future/out-of-order records. | 7 | 4 | 6 | **168** | Inject the clock into every domain service/entity creation path, standardise on UTC instants plus explicit UK business dates, and add boundary tests and clock-skew monitoring. |
+
+## Priority and ownership
+
+Immediate release blockers are FMEA-01 (fraud bypass), FMEA-02 (scheme timeout ambiguity), FMEA-03/FMEA-04 (ledger
+consistency and idempotent recovery), and FMEA-08/FMEA-09 (authoritative funds and closure controls). The Accounts squad
+should own the state-machine, tenant isolation, scheduling and observability work. Fraud owns screening decisions and
+review SLAs; Finance owns ledger reconciliation and posting exceptions; Payments/Scheme Operations owns scheme status
+queries and settlement breaks; Regulatory Reporting owns statement completeness; Compliance owns the data-exposure
+controls.
+
+The required verification command for changes is:
+
+```text
+mvn -o -q test
+```
